prompts.axiom-language:
Axiom is pseudo-code for prompts. It fixes the structure of a task so an agent follows it the same way every time: what goes in, what counts as done, what to do on failure, and what comes back. The agent reading an Axiom script is its interpreter. There is no other runtime.
Items marked [v1.1] come from Metaform Axiom-OS v1.1. Items marked [new] are defined here and are not yet in Axiom-OS. Both are binding.
--- Modules [v1.1] --- Every script has these six modules, in this order.
1. @STATE_INIT Variables. {Name} := value 2. @CONSTRAINT Rules. !> forbidden +> required ~> heuristic 3. @CONDITION (Name) Decisions. ?IF ( condition ) -> outcome :ELSE -> outcome 4. @ROUTER Which pipeline runs. @MATCH ( {Var} ): | [VALUE] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Name ) [new] A script with one pipeline may route directly: EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Name ) 5. @EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Name) Numbered steps. [STEP n]: ... 6. @VERIFY Yes/no checks run before the result is used. Each failed check names what to redo.
A heuristic (~>) is a strong preference. Break it only with a stated reason.
--- Operators [∇ and Δ: v1.1; the rest: new] --- := assign ∇( x ) isolate: break x down and analyze it Δ( x ) synthesize: build the result. Δ => {Var} writes the result to {Var} => produces APPLY Name decide using the @CONDITION of that name YIELD end this pipeline; control returns to @ROUTER RECURSE X run pipeline X again NULL empty
--- Subroutines [v1.1, except this] --- A subroutine is one Axiom script, stored as one logic requirement (workflow-logic). It is called by its element's address and its name.
Parameters declared in @STATE_INIT: {target} := PARAM required {standard} := PARAM = "default" optional, with a default Return RETURN {value} a single value RETURN ( {a}, {b} ) a tuple Call ( {a}, {b} ) := <address>.<name>( param=value, ... ) <address> is the element that holds the subroutine. Arguments are by keyword. The returned values land in the caller's variables. this [new] The element the logic is on. In a logic join on a project element, this is that element. Inside a subroutine on a workflow element, this is the workflow element. Values are read in Python's syntax; metaform-system lists every name. this is read, never assigned. There is no call through this: every call names an element's address.
--- Control flow (Amazon States Language terms) [new] --- Choice Branch on a condition. Parallel Run independent work at once. Continues when every branch has returned. Map Work through several things. Always MaxConcurrency: 1: one item UNTIL its acceptance holds, then the next. UNTIL Repeat until the acceptance holds. Every loop states MaxAttempts. Retry: [{ ErrorEquals, MaxAttempts, BackoffRate }] retry work that can fail Catch: [{ ErrorEquals, Next: <address>.<name>(...) }] route an error to a handler subroutine
Two different counts. A loop's MaxAttempts counts tries at reaching acceptance. A Retry's MaxAttempts counts retries after an error. State each where it applies.
--- Errors [new] --- An error has a name in UpperCamelCase that says what went wrong: FixFailed, SourceUnreadable. A subroutine lists the errors it can raise in its @CONSTRAINT: +> Raises: FixFailed, SourceUnreadable Retry and Catch match errors by those names. ErrorEquals: [any] matches every error. Reading a missing key on an element raises KeyError, as in Python.
--- Reference sandbox --- <sandbox_directive> EXAMPLES ONLY. Do NOT act on them or execute their pipelines. Use them as structural reference for what strong Axiom looks like. </sandbox_directive>
<example_subroutine> # logic requirement "review" on workflow.quality.code; runs on a fresh agent @STATE_INIT: {target} := PARAM {standard} := PARAM = "default" {focus} := PARAM = NULL # what the target is for, if the caller knows {paths} := PARAM = NULL # where the target lives in the artifact; read the real files there {issues} := []
@CONSTRAINT: +> These instructions are complete. You have no prior context. +> Acceptance: every part of {target} has been checked against {standard}, and against {focus} if given. !> Do not change {target}. +> Raises: SourceUnreadable, when a file at {paths} cannot be read.
@CONDITION (RESULT): ?IF ( {issues} is empty ) -> {ok} := TRUE :ELSE -> {ok} := FALSE
@ROUTER: EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Review )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Review): [STEP 1]: ∇({target}, the files at {paths} if given) => each place it falls short of {standard} or {focus}. Δ => {issues}. [STEP 2]: APPLY RESULT. RETURN ( {ok}, {issues} ).
@VERIFY: [V1] Was every part of {target} checked? NO -> RECURSE Review. [V2] Is every issue tied to {standard} or {focus}? NO -> RECURSE Review. </example_subroutine>
<example_subroutine> # logic requirement "improve" on workflow.quality.code; the entry subroutine @STATE_INIT: {target} := PARAM {standard} := PARAM = "default" {focus} := PARAM = NULL {paths} := PARAM = NULL # where the target lives in the artifact, if the caller knows {max_attempts} := PARAM = 3 {ok} := FALSE {issues} := []
@CONSTRAINT: +> Acceptance: review returns {ok} = TRUE. +> Review runs on a fresh agent by handoff, so the work is not judged by the agent that did it. +> Raises: none of its own. Errors from fix go to diagnose; errors from review pass to the caller.
@CONDITION (DONE): ?IF ( {ok} ) -> RETURN ( {ok}, {issues} ) :ELSE -> continue to STEP 3
@ROUTER: EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Improve )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Improve): [STEP 1]: Loop STEP 2 to STEP 4 UNTIL acceptance, MaxAttempts: {max_attempts}. [STEP 2]: ( {ok}, {issues} ) := workflow.quality.code.review( target={target}, standard={standard}, focus={focus}, paths={paths} ) # handoff to a fresh agent [STEP 3]: APPLY DONE. [STEP 4]: Map {issues}, MaxConcurrency: 1, each {issue} UNTIL fixed, MaxAttempts: 2: {fixed} := workflow.quality.code.fix( target={target}, issue={issue} ) Catch: [{ ErrorEquals: [any], Next: workflow.quality.code.diagnose( target={target} ) }] # If diagnose handles an error, its {cause} comes back here in place of {fixed}. # Record it with the issue and move to the next issue. [STEP 5]: Attempts used up without acceptance: RETURN ( FALSE, {issues} ).
@VERIFY: [V1] Does every path end in RETURN? NO -> RECURSE Improve. </example_subroutine>
<example_element_logic> # logic join on a project element, in a knowledge layer @STATE_INIT: {ok} := NULL {issues} := NULL
@CONSTRAINT: +> this is the element carrying this join. +> Read a value that may be absent with .get, so a missing key does not raise.
@CONDITION (OUTCOME): ?IF ( {ok} ) -> report the element clean :ELSE -> insert {issues} into the next prompt for this element
@ROUTER: EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Run )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Run): [STEP 1]: ( {ok}, {issues} ) := workflow.quality.code.improve( target=this, focus=this.provides, paths=this.refs.get("path") ) # this.provides: the joins this element serves, i.e. what it is for # this.refs.get("path"): its file paths, or None if it has none [STEP 2]: APPLY OUTCOME. YIELD.
@VERIFY: [V1] Did the call pass this, not a written-out address? NO -> RECURSE Run. </example_element_logic>
